---
/**
 * foodcraft / CredentialsBL — "How we keep standards." (Kalinaw Coffee).
 *
 * ROLE: the design's CREDENTIALS band. An eyebrow, one display headline, and
 * under it a single hairline-boxed RAIL divided into four equal cells on a
 * white card ground. Each cell opens with an ITALIC RUST serif numeral, then an
 * 11px tracked-caps title, then a 13px sentence. It is the café's standing on
 * record — direct trade, barista training, a sanitation certificate — which is
 * exactly why every word of it is owner-typed and none of it is drawn here.
 *
 * DESIGN FIDELITY — every value below is lifted from the CREDENTIALS element of
 * "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html" and mapped hex → token (R1). No literal hex:
 *   eyebrow   10px / .42em / 600 / #B4541F ...... the wrapper's .kick, exactly
 *   headline  clamp(31px,4vw,54px) / 1.08 / #241A12, margin .26em 0 36px
 *             ................................... the wrapper's .h2 + a local
 *             top margin; the 36px moves onto the rail (see DECISION 2)
 *   rail      4 × minmax(0,1fr), 1px #E9DFCF box, 16px radius, overflow hidden
 *             ................................... var(--line), var(--r-lg)
 *   cell      padding 32px 24px on #FFFFFF, 1px #E9DFCF border-right
 *             ................................... var(--card), var(--line)
 *   numeral   Fraunces ITALIC 21px #B4541F + 13px of air
 *             ................................... the wrapper's .fig, made
 *             italic and rust locally (see DECISION 3), var(--accent)
 *   title     11px / .2em / 600 / #241A12, margin 0 0 9px
 *             ................................... var(--sans), var(--ink)
 *   body      13px / 1.7 / #948373, margin 0 .... var(--muted)
 * The accent is the one token the design itself exposes as configurable, and
 * therefore the one an admin scheme is welcome to move.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (all four are declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts under section id 'credentials' — nothing here is a
 * dead hook):
 *   credentials.tag ................ the eyebrow
 *   credentials.headline ........... the h2, set:html so an owner can put the
 *                                    design's italic rust <em> anywhere inside
 *                                    their own sentence
 *   credentials.items.${i}.title ... the tracked-caps cell title
 *   credentials.items.${i}.body .... the sentence under it. CANONICAL key is
 *                                    `body`: the sidebar writes rows as
 *                                    {title, body}, and astro-builder's
 *                                    normalizeBlock only COPIES body into desc
 *                                    when desc is empty (it never deletes
 *                                    body), so the value reads from either —
 *                                    but the hook must spell `body`, which is
 *                                    what the schema declares. CredentialsBI
 *                                    spells `desc`; that hook is dead. The
 *                                    reader below still accepts desc/detail/
 *                                    description as INPUT, for sites saved
 *                                    before the schema settled, while the hook
 *                                    it writes stays `body`.
 *
 * DERIVED, and therefore carrying NO data-field:
 *   · the numeral (01, 02, 03…) is built from the DISPLAY index, so the rail
 *     always counts without gaps however many blank rows were dropped, and an
 *     edit can never be misdirected into a counter (R6). The design's own data
 *     is zero-padded to two digits ('01'…'04'); padStart reproduces that.
 *     aria-hidden — it is ornament, and the title beside it is the content.
 *   · the track count: four tracks at four rows and above, and exactly
 *     rows.length below that, so two credentials fill the rail's width instead
 *     of hanging off its left edge. Above four the rows wrap and the leftover
 *     of the last row stays card-toned, which keeps the rail one solid object
 *     rather than a grid with a hole in it.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. content.credentials has no derived
 * fallback whatsoever: astro-builder passes `c.credentials ?? undefined`, and
 * derive-content-defaults never invents a credential. This band therefore
 * exists only where an owner put something on record. Consequences, all
 * deliberate:
 *   · with no filled row the WHOLE <section> is omitted — no eyebrow, no
 *     headline, no rail, no padding (R5: the gate counts the ROWS, never the
 *     tag or the headline; keepHead can backfill those two from a derived
 *     block, and a gate that counted them would ship a heading over nothing).
 *   · title and body are guarded independently and OUTSIDE their tags, so a
 *     half-filled row prints only what was typed — never an empty <h3> holding
 *     open 9px of margin under the numeral. Those guards are TRIM-AWARE only
 *     because the reader coerces and trims once, before anything else touches
 *     the value (see the note above it): a title of "   " is truthy, and
 *     guarded raw it would ride through the row filter on its sibling's body
 *     and print exactly that empty heading.
 *   · the head guard sits outside its wrapper div too, so an owner who typed
 *     neither field gets no empty box above the rail.
 *   · the design's demo strings ("Direct trade", "Roast date on every bag") are
 *     the DESIGNER filling in their own template. A café that never claimed SCA
 *     training must not have this page claim it for them.
 *   · NO seal, shield, ribbon, stamp, tick or badge of any kind, and no logo
 *     strip. A drawn badge is a visual claim of certification the owner never
 *     made; the design draws none either. The numeral and the hairline are the
 *     whole ornament.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED (so none of it reads as an oversight):
 *   · NO onPage() anchor-remap helper and no id. 'credentials' is not one of
 *     PageBL's ids (menu about gallery location faq main), nothing on the page
 *     links here, and — decisively — this section emits NO href of any kind:
 *     the design gives CREDENTIALS no link and no button. A remap helper here
 *     would be a dead export with nothing to remap, and R7 exists to stop an
 *     anchor pointing at an id this page does not have, not to require a helper
 *     where there is no anchor. CredentialsBK records the same decision. If a
 *     later revision adds an action to this band, copy the helper from
 *     ServicesBL/HeaderBL wholesale rather than hand-rolling a shorter one.
 *   · no star row. R2's rating rule is real but has nothing to bind to: a
 *     credential carries no numeric rating field, and five drawn stars would be
 *     a fabricated claim. The design draws stars in TESTIMONIALS, not here.
 *   · no `band-dark`. The design keeps CREDENTIALS on the cream page ground and
 *     puts the white card tone INSIDE the rail. Banding the section would erase
 *     the rail entirely — it is a white box on cream, and on espresso it would
 *     read as a completely different component.
 *   · no data-block marker: editorBridge only needs one where two hook prefixes
 *     share a <section>. Everything here is credentials.*, so the Blocks toggle
 *     already resolves to it by hook weight.
 *   · no .plate / .ph: the design gives this band no photograph, so there is no
 *     image slot to offer and no role="img" to name (R9).
 *
 * FOUR DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE QUESTION:
 *   1. VERTICAL RHYTHM IS THE WRAPPER'S. The design writes `padding:96px 32px 0`
 *      on this section; PageBL's shared `section { padding: clamp(56px,7.6vw,96px) 0 }`
 *      already tops out at exactly that 96px, and `.wrap`'s min(1240px,92vw)
 *      reproduces the 1240px measure. So this file adds no section padding of
 *      its own — changing it here alone would desynchronise this band from its
 *      fourteen siblings.
 *   2. THE HEADLINE MEASURE IS RELEASED, and the design's 36px bottom margin
 *      moves ONTO THE RAIL. The design's CREDENTIALS h2 carries no max-width
 *      and sits on one line at 1240px, where the wrapper's `.h2 { max-width:
 *      24ch }` would wrap it, so `max-width: none` is set locally — size, face,
 *      colour, the italic rust <em> and both shared mobile passes still come
 *      from .h2. The 36px becomes the rail's `margin-top` so that a site with
 *      an eyebrow but no headline, or with neither, gets the right gap instead
 *      of a phantom one under an element that is not there.
 *   3. THE NUMERAL IS ITALIC AND RUST, which .fig alone is not. The wrapper's
 *      .fig gives it Fraunces, weight 400, tabular lining figures and line-height
 *      1 — all of which the design wants — but pins colour to --ink and leaves
 *      the face upright. The design's numeral is Fraunces italic in the accent,
 *      matching the headline's <em>, so this file restates exactly those two
 *      properties plus the 21px size. Fraunces ships a real italic at 500 (the
 *      wrapper loads 1,9..144,500), so nothing here is a synthesised oblique.
 *   4. THE CELL DIVIDER IS A BORDER, never a box-shadow. Grid items paint in
 *      order-modified document order, each atomically (CSS Grid §6.2 — they
 *      paint as inline-blocks), so a hairline cast to the right as a shadow
 *      lands on the next cell's border box and is buried under that cell's
 *      opaque --card ground; one cast downwards is buried by the row below or
 *      clipped by the rail's overflow:hidden under the last row. A
 *      shadow-drawn divider renders nowhere. The trailing hairline on the last
 *      cell of a row is the design's own drawing — its sc-for puts border-right
 *      on EVERY cell inside the same hairline box — so it is reproduced rather
 *      than tidied into a border-left-except-first, which is a different
 *      drawing.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style>, no is:global. The headline is injected with set:html,
 * so scoped rules cannot reach an <em> inside it; its italic rust styling comes
 * from PageBL's global `:where(.h2 em)` and needs nothing local. (No literal
 * end-style-tag appears in any comment below, here or in the stylesheet.)
 *
 * CONTRAST on the rail's white --card ground. PageBL's frontmatter records the
 * palette's pairs; the two that matter here are ink/card 15.3 (the titles) and
 * accent/paper 5.31 (the eyebrow and the numerals — fractionally better on the
 * brighter --card than on --paper). The 13px body is --muted, which the wrapper
 * records at 3.86 on --paper and which is likewise a shade better on white:
 * BELOW AA either way. It is the design's own value, PageBL already documents
 * the decision and the one-line fix (--muted → #7C6C5C, a wrapper-level and
 * owner-level call), so this file ships the design rather than silently
 * re-deriving a palette the owner did not ask to change.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBL hands it to all fifteen sections
// uniformly. This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never
// destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// The schema declares 'credentials.items' with fallbackPaths ['credentials'],
// so the stored value is legitimately EITHER {tag, headline, items:[…]} or a
// bare array. Both shapes are read.
const cred = content.credentials || {};

// COERCE, THEN TRIM, ONCE AT THE READER — the house rule the BL siblings share
// (WhyBL, TestimonialsBL, FaqBL). Two reasons, both real:
//   · a value that arrives as a NUMBER — an owner who typed `2024` into a
//     credential title, or an older AI save — has no .trim(), so calling it in
//     the row filter below would throw and take the whole build with it.
//   · trimming here is what makes every guard downstream trim-AWARE. A title of
//     "   " is truthy: guarded untrimmed it rides through the row filter on its
//     sibling's body and prints an EMPTY <h3> holding open 9px of margin under
//     the numeral — precisely the empty element R2 forbids.
// Trimming is not supplying content: the owner's words are untouched, only the
// whitespace around them.
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '';

// The eyebrow is credentials.tag ONLY. The design's "Credentials" literal is
// the designer filling in their own template; it is not this file's to type.
const tag = str(cred.tag).trim();
const headline = str(cred.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  title?: unknown; label?: unknown;
  body?: unknown; desc?: unknown; detail?: unknown; description?: unknown;
};
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(cred.items)
  ? cred.items
  : (Array.isArray(cred) ? cred : []);
const titleOf = (it: Item): string => (str(it.title).trim() || str(it.label).trim());
// Reads the aliases as INPUT (older saves, and normalizeBlock's desc copy) but
// the data-field emitted below always spells the canonical `body`. `||` rather
// than `??` deliberately: normalizeBlock copies body into desc whenever desc is
// merely absent, so a row saved with body:'' and a real desc must fall through
// rather than stop on the empty string.
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string =>
  (str(it.body).trim() || str(it.desc).trim() || str(it.detail).trim() || str(it.description).trim());

// R6 — a row an owner added but never filled would otherwise draw a cell, a
// numeral and 32px of padding over nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts
// as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index, so every data-field still writes to
// the real array slot: removing credential 2 must never redirect an edit into
// credential 3.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ ...it, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(titleOf(r) || bodyOf(r)));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
// The design's rail is four tracks. Below four rows we narrow to the row count
// so the rail stays a full-width object; at four and above the rows wrap into
// four and the leftover stays card-toned.
const cols = Math.min(rows.length, 4);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="cred">
    <div class="wrap">
      {hasHead && (
        <div class="cred-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="credentials.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 cred-h" data-field="credentials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class:list={['cred-rail', 'reveal', `cols-${cols}`, hasHead && 'has-head']} data-delay="1">
        {rows.map((it, k: number) => (
          <div class="cred-cell">
            {/* DISPLAY index — the rail reads 01, 02, 03 however many blank rows
                were dropped. No data-field: it is a counter, never stored. ONE
                literal aria-hidden and never a conditional one beside it (R9). */}
            <span class="fig cred-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {titleOf(it) && <h3 class="cred-t" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.title`}>{titleOf(it)}</h3>}
            {bodyOf(it) && <p class="cred-p" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.body`}>{bodyOf(it)}</p>}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The section keeps PageBL's shared vertical padding and the cream page
     ground: the design puts the white card tone INSIDE the rail, not under the
     band. No band-dark here. */
  .cred { min-width: 0; }

  .cred-head { min-width: 0; }

  /* The eyebrow IS the wrapper's .kick — nothing about the design's span is
     restated, so it cannot fork from the other fourteen sections. The one thing
     ADDED is a wrap rule the wrapper never sets: .kick is an inline-block
     carrying .42em of tracking, so one long unbroken owner word is unusually
     wide for its character count and would push the document sideways at 360px
     (R8). Same rule, same reason, as AboutBL / FaqBL / AreaBL. */
  .cred-head .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* The design's headline margin is `.26em 0 36px`. Only the top half lives
     here; the 36px moves onto the rail so a site with no headline never gets a
     gap under an element that is not there.
     max-width: none — the design's CREDENTIALS h2 carries NO measure cap and
     draws on one line at 1240px, which the wrapper's `.h2 { max-width: 24ch }`
     would break to two. Size, face, colour and the italic rust <em> still come
     from .h2; only the cap is released. overflow-wrap rides with it so a pasted
     unbroken string still cannot widen the wrap above 760px. */
  .cred-h { margin: .26em 0 0; max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* THE RAIL — one hairline box with the cells flush inside it and the design's
     16px corners clipping them. The track count is emitted as a CLASS rather
     than a custom property because repeat() will not take a calc() or a min()
     for its count, and a class is what a breakpoint can override. The rail
     carries --card itself so that a fifth-and-later row's empty tail stays part
     of the same white object instead of showing cream through the grid. */
  .cred-rail {
    display: grid;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background-color: var(--card);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .cred-rail.has-head { margin-top: 36px; }
  .cols-1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .cols-2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .cols-3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .cols-4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  .cred-cell {
    padding: 32px 24px;
    min-width: 0;
    background-color: var(--card);
    /* The design's own declaration, verbatim: a hairline on the RIGHT of every
       repeated cell, including the last of a row, inside the rail's own box.
       See DECISION 4 in the frontmatter for why this is a border and not a
       shadow, and why the trailing hairline is reproduced rather than tidied. */
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  /* Only reachable when an owner puts MORE than the design's four credentials
     on record: the rows wrap, and each row after the first needs the horizontal
     hairline the design never had to draw. At this width wrapping can only
     happen in a 4-track rail (below four rows the rail narrows to the row
     count), so n+5 is the whole rule. Restated under each breakpoint below,
     because the track count changes there. */
  .cred-cell:nth-child(n + 5) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }

  /* Fraunces italic 21px rust, then 13px of air. .fig supplies the face, the
     weight, the tabular lining figures and line-height 1; the italic and the
     accent are the design's and are restated here (DECISION 3). */
  .cred-n {
    display: block;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 21px;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: 13px;
  }

  /* PageBL sets :where(h1,h2,h3,h4) to Fraunces 400 at line-height 1.08 — these
     titles are Space Grotesk 600 tracked caps, so face, weight, size, tracking,
     transform and colour are all restated. Line-height is 1.35 rather than the
     wrapper's 1.08: the design's titles sit on one line, where leading is
     invisible, but "Roast date on every bag" already wraps in a 24px-padded
     quarter track and the cap-heights of two tracked-caps lines must not touch. */
  .cred-t {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.35; letter-spacing: .2em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 9px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* PageBL's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1); qualifying with the cell
     class puts this above it so the design's zero margin actually lands. */
  .cred-cell .cred-p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 900 · R8. A 4-up rail of 11px caps at .2em over a 13px body is
        unreadable once the track drops under ~200px, so 3 and 4 tracks fold to
        2. One and two already fit and are left alone. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cols-3, .cols-4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    /* Two up: the interior vertical rule is now the odd cell's right border,
       and every cell from the third on gains a horizontal one. The design's
       32px 24px padding is untouched — a ~420px track is not pressing on it. */
    .cred-cell:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  }

  /* ── 520 · one column, and nothing may push the page sideways: titles and
        bodies already break anywhere, and the rail is a grid of minmax(0,1fr)
        tracks, so a long unbroken word cannot widen it past the wrap. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .cred-rail { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    /* 36px under a clamp(27px,7.2vw,40px) headline is proportionally most of a
       line; 28px keeps the design's ratio at phone scale. */
    .cred-rail.has-head { margin-top: 28px; }
    /* ONLY the VERTICAL padding moves off the design's 32px 24px, and only
       because stacking spends it once per row. The 24px HORIZONTAL is the
       design's and stays — the wrap is 92vw (~331px at 360), so nothing is
       pressing on it. The trailing right hairline is dropped here: one-up it
       would double the rail's own right border down the whole height of the
       band instead of dividing anything. */
    .cred-cell { padding: 26px 24px; border-right: 0; }
    .cred-cell:nth-child(n + 2) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  }
</style>
